            {
              "id": "eb2cd348-d530-3f57-93ab-407dc81c48a7",
              "name": "Detected Vulnerability in C Function (strlen)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur when using the `strlen` function in C++. This function returns the length of a string, but it does not include the null terminator. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` can lead to a buffer overflow, causing the program to crash or leading to execution of arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code, `sprintf` is used to append a string to `(*kv)[*kvnum].name`. If the length of the string exceeds the size of the buffer, a buffer overflow can occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always ensure that your strings are null-terminated.\n- Use functions that limit the number of characters written to a buffer, such as `snprintf` instead of `sprintf`.\n- Always check the length of the string and the size of the buffer before copying or appending strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with the `snprintf` function, which includes a parameter for the maximum number of characters to be written to the buffer:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name + strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name) - strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), \"[%\" PRId64 \"]\", y);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `sprintf` and `snprintf`\n- `string.h` for `strlen`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-120",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "LOW",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-120"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "827e3500-579e-3d93-ac37-6a17d28e3ec8",
              "name": "Detected Prohibited C Function",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the `fname` parameter is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to perform proper input validation and error checking. In the case of `fopen`, it is important to ensure that the `fname` parameter is a valid and secure file path, and that the file is properly handled after opening.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(fname, std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the `std::ifstream` class from the C++ Standard Library is used instead of `fopen`. This class provides a safer and more convenient interface for file I/O.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-676",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-676"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1a4eaa8-48df-33c3-b7c2-fd9ccca7ea32",
              "name": "Detected Vulnerability in C Function (strlen)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur when using the `strlen` function in C++. This function does not check for the end of the string and can lead to reading beyond the allocated memory if the string is not null-terminated. This can lead to undefined behavior, including crashes, data corruption, and code execution.\n\nIn the provided code, the `sprintf` function is used to append a string to `(*kv)[*kvnum].name`. The `strlen` function is used to calculate the length of the existing string, and the result is used as an offset for the destination pointer. If the string `(*kv)[*kvnum].name` is not null-terminated, `strlen` will read beyond the allocated memory, leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings after you have finished modifying them.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer alternatives to `sprintf`, such as `snprintf`, which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be written to the destination string, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name + strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name) - strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), \".smn[%\" PRId64 \"]\", cur_smn);\n```\n\nIn this version, `snprintf` is used instead of `sprintf`, and the maximum number of characters to be written is calculated as the size of the destination string minus the length of the existing string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `snprintf`\n- `string.h` for `strlen`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-120",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "LOW",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-120"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f4636ae9-3aeb-37f9-a8f2-67b5480d36a9",
              "name": "Detected Vulnerability in C Function (strlen)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur when using the `strlen` function in C++. This function does not check for the end of the string and can lead to reading beyond the allocated memory if the string is not null-terminated. This can lead to undefined behavior, including crashes, data corruption, and code execution.\n\nIn the provided code, the `sprintf` function is used to append a string to `(*kv)[*kvnum].name`. If the size of the appended string exceeds the size of the allocated memory for `(*kv)[*kvnum].name`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated and that you do not exceed the allocated memory when appending to a string. You should also consider using safer alternatives to `sprintf`, such as `snprintf`, which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be written to the output string, thus preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name + strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name) - strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), \".instance[%\" PRId64 \"]\", cur_instance);\n```\n\nThis code uses `snprintf` instead of `sprintf` and specifies the maximum number of characters to be written as the size of the allocated memory minus the current length of the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `snprintf`\n- `string.h` for `strlen`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
