            {
              "id": "133d62f5-dedc-3991-aabb-fabb956b7c17",
              "name": "Detected Prohibited C Function",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to implement proper checks and controls to ensure that the function is used securely. For `fopen`, it is important to validate and control the file path that is used, and to handle errors properly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using C++ file streams:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(sysfs_path.c_str(), std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
