            {
              "id": "a7cece13-1c56-3fdf-a0c9-f78ec0f12833",
              "name": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code performs a memory copy operation without verifying that either the source or destination pointers are non-null. The risk is that if either pointer is null, the program will exhibit undefined behavior, which can lead to crashes or security vulnerabilities. The analysis did not detect any explicit checks to ensure that the source (cache.data.data()) or destination (p_binary_data) pointers are valid before the copy. Additionally, the function does not appear to use a length value derived from the actual capacity of the destination buffer, nor does it have any guard conditions to prevent misuse. These factors indicate a real risk that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null, and that the size to copy is valid. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (p_binary_data != nullptr && cache.data.data() != nullptr && b_size > 0) {\n    std::memcpy(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n}\n```\n\nIf either pointer can be null as part of normal operation, handle this case appropriately (e.g., by returning an error or skipping the copy).\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to enforce that neither pointer is null and that the size is valid, you can use assertions or error handling to catch issues early:\n\n```cpp\nassert(p_binary_data != nullptr);\nassert(cache.data.data() != nullptr);\nassert(b_size > 0);\nstd::memcpy(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n```\n\nReplace assertions with proper error handling if this is production code.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
