            {
              "id": "6612adb1-9c85-32d8-a634-43fafeddf460",
              "name": "\"Prohibited C Function Identified\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, memory leaks, or other unexpected behavior. In this case, the function `std::localtime` is identified as a potential vulnerability sink. This function converts a given time to local time, based on the system's time zone settings. However, it is not thread-safe, meaning that if it is called from multiple threads simultaneously, it can cause data races and undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use thread-safe alternatives to `std::localtime`. In C++, this could be the `std::localtime_s` function (on Windows) or `localtime_r` function (on POSIX systems). These functions take an additional parameter, which is a pointer to a `std::tm` structure where the result is stored. This makes them safe to use in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can replace `std::localtime` with `std::localtime_s` or `localtime_r`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::time_t timer;\nstd::tm tm_result;\n\n#ifdef _WIN32\nstd::localtime_s(&tm_result, &timer);\n#else\nlocaltime_r(&timer, &tm_result);\n#endif\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<ctime>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-676",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-676"
                ]
              }
