            {
              "id": "e3f3f5b3-a65e-3f9d-8608-08651b8746cb",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's capacity is not known or proven to be sufficient for the copy operation. The code copies a string literal into a destination buffer using a maximum size macro minus one, but there is no evidence that the destination buffer is at least as large as the specified count. The use of a string literal as the source reduces the risk of unexpected data, but does not mitigate the risk of buffer overflow if the destination is too small. There are no explicit checks or guards to ensure the destination buffer is large enough, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The analysis did not detect any risk of overlapping memory regions or null pointers for source or destination, but the main concern remains the lack of proof regarding the destination buffer's size.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the destination buffer is at least as large as the maximum size specified in the copy operation. If possible, statically assert or check at runtime that the buffer is large enough. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), \"Unknown event received\", MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis ensures that the destination buffer is always null-terminated, even if the source string is longer than the buffer size. If the structure definition allows, consider using a fixed-size character array for `message` and ensure its size matches `MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE`.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you cannot guarantee the size of the destination buffer, add a runtime check before copying to ensure safety:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(data_item->message) >= MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), \"Unknown event received\", MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: buffer too small\n}\n```\n\nThis approach prevents buffer overflows by verifying the buffer size at runtime before performing the copy.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 3\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-120",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-120",
                  "CWE-119"
                ]
              }
