            {
              "id": "af50df7a-f08a-31a7-aca5-e0ef24fb0dc2",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible null pointers, and the automated verdict confirms this. The code copies a string into a buffer using a function that does not check if the source pointer is null. The source string is generated by calling the c_str() method on a string stream, which could potentially return a null pointer if the string is empty or uninitialized. There are no explicit checks in the code to ensure that the source pointer is valid before performing the copy. The destination buffer is an address-of expression, which is always non-null, so the main risk is with the source pointer. The absence of a guard or explicit null check for the source pointer increases the likelihood of undefined behavior if a null pointer is encountered. This is a common source of security and stability issues in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the source pointer is not null. You can do this by checking if the result of `final_message.str().c_str()` is not null before calling the copy function. For example:\n\n```cpp\nconst std::string msg_str = final_message.str();\nconst char* src_ptr = msg_str.c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle the error case, e.g., set message to empty string\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs if the source pointer is valid, preventing undefined behavior from a null pointer dereference.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, since `std::string::c_str()` is guaranteed to return a valid pointer (never null) for a valid `std::string` object, ensure that `final_message` is always properly initialized and not in a moved-from or invalid state before this code is reached. If you are certain that `final_message` is always valid, you may add an assertion to document this assumption:\n\n```cpp\nconst std::string msg_str = final_message.str();\nassert(!msg_str.empty()); // or assert(msg_str.c_str() != nullptr);\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), msg_str.c_str(), MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n// Optionally, ensure null-termination\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis documents the assumption and will catch violations in debug builds.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
