            {
              "id": "a1c8b431-a90a-3a48-88f0-e85170f0ed27",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null source pointer, and the verdict for this call site is marked as genuine. The code copies a string from a potentially null source pointer into a destination buffer using a function that does not perform null checks. The source is derived from a C-style string, and there is no explicit check to ensure it is not null before copying. The destination is a non-null address-of expression, so the main risk is from the source. There are no guards or explicit null-termination after the copy, which increases the risk of undefined behavior if the source is null. The absence of overlap risk and the use of a named constant for the copy length do not mitigate the null pointer risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the source pointer is not null. If it is null, handle the error or assign a default value. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle null source pointer, e.g., set message to empty string\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to ensure safety and avoid manual null-termination, consider using a safer string copy function that guarantees null-termination, such as `strlcpy` (if available), or wrap the logic to always null-terminate after copying. Always check for null pointers before copying.\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0';\n}\n\n// If strlcpy is available:\n// strlcpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr ? src_ptr : \"\", MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE);\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
