              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null source pointer, and the verdict for this call site is marked as genuine. The code copies a string from a potentially null source pointer into a destination buffer using a function that does not check for null pointers. The source is derived from a C-style string, which increases the risk if the string object is empty or uninitialized. There are no explicit checks to ensure the source pointer is not null before the copy operation. The destination is an address-of expression, so it is guaranteed to be non-null, but the lack of a null check on the source is a significant risk. No explicit buffer size or null-termination handling is present, and there is no guard to prevent copying from a null pointer. This combination of factors makes the issue genuine and actionable.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to verify that the result of `final_message.str().c_str()` is not null before calling the copy function. If the source is null, handle the error appropriately (e.g., set the destination to an empty string or log an error).\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // or handle error\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs if the source pointer is valid, and the destination is always null-terminated.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you are certain that `final_message.str()` will never return a null pointer, you can add an assertion to document this assumption and catch violations during development:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nassert(src_ptr != nullptr);\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis approach is less robust in production but can help catch issues during testing.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
