              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null pointer being passed as the source to the string copy operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as genuine. The source of the data is a C-style string, and there is no explicit check to ensure it is not null before the copy operation. The destination is a non-null address, but the absence of a null check on the source means that if the source is null, the program may crash or exhibit undefined behavior. There are no additional safety guards or explicit null-termination after the copy, further increasing the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy operation, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the source is null. For example:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // or handle error as appropriate\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the destination buffer is not written with a null source, and also explicitly null-terminates the destination buffer to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you expect that the source may be null and want to provide a default message or handle the error, you can do so as follows:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr == nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), \"<no message>\", MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach ensures the destination buffer always contains a valid, null-terminated string, even if the source is null.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
