              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null pointer being passed as the source to the string copy operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The source of the copy is a C-style string, which increases the risk if it is not properly checked for null before use. There are no explicit checks in the code to ensure that the source pointer is not null, and the destination is a pointer type without a known fixed capacity, which further increases the risk. The absence of a null-terminator assignment after the copy and the lack of a guard condition to validate the source pointer reinforce the assessment that this is a genuine vulnerability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the source is null. For example:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // or handle error as appropriate\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs if the source is valid, and the destination is always null-terminated to prevent potential string overflows or unterminated strings.\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "79f269c2-0568-3140-8ae4-fb91145c9f2d",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible null pointer dereference, and the verdict for this call site is marked as genuine. The code copies a string into a buffer using a function that does not check if the source pointer is null. The source is derived from a C-style string, which may be null if the string object is empty or uninitialized, and there is no explicit check to ensure it is valid before use. The destination is a non-null address, so the main risk is with the source. There are no explicit guards or null-termination after the copy, which further increases the risk. The probability of this being a false positive is very low due to these factors.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the source pointer is not null. You can add a check to verify that the result of `final_message.str().c_str()` is not null before calling the copy function. If it is null, handle the error appropriately (e.g., set the destination to an empty string or log an error).\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // or handle error\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs if the source is valid, and the destination is always null-terminated to prevent buffer over-read or undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you are certain that `final_message.str()` will never be null, you can add an assertion to document this assumption and catch programming errors during development:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nassert(src_ptr != nullptr);\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis approach is only safe if you are confident that the source will never be null in production builds.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
