            {
              "id": "75a8d450-82c5-3f82-b7b9-bc7a902655df",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's capacity is not known or proven to be sufficient for the copy operation. The code copies a string literal into a buffer using a maximum size macro minus one, but there is no evidence that the buffer is at least as large as the macro value. The use of a string literal as the source reduces the risk of unexpected data, but does not mitigate the risk of buffer overflow if the destination is too small. There are no explicit checks or guards to ensure the destination buffer is large enough, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The analysis did not detect any risk of overlapping memory regions or null pointers for source or destination, but the main concern is the lack of proof about the destination's size. This makes the finding high risk and actionable.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo remediate this issue, ensure that the destination buffer is at least as large as the maximum size macro used in the copy operation. If possible, statically assert or check at runtime that the buffer is large enough. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), \"Event type None received\", MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf the structure definition is under your control, ensure that the message field is defined as a character array of at least MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE bytes. If not, consider using safer string copy functions or adding runtime checks before copying.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you cannot guarantee the size of the destination buffer, add a runtime check before copying to prevent buffer overflow:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(data_item->message) >= MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), \"Event type None received\", MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: buffer too small\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
