            {
              "id": "e6c1ef5d-5a8d-36c8-ae99-94952fcaca2a",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible null pointer dereference for either the source or destination buffer. The code copies a string from a C++ string's c_str() method into a character buffer using strncpy, but there are no explicit checks to ensure that either the source (vendor_name.c_str()) or the destination (name) pointers are non-null before the operation. If either pointer is null, this will result in undefined behavior, which can lead to crashes or security vulnerabilities. The use of a C string as the source and the absence of overlap risk slightly increase the chance of a false positive, but the lack of null checks is a strong indicator of a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the string copy operation, add explicit checks to ensure that both the destination buffer and the source string pointer are not null. This prevents undefined behavior if either pointer is null.\n\n```cpp\nif (name != nullptr && vendor_name.c_str() != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), len-1);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination:\n    name[len-1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nIf either pointer can be null as part of normal program logic, handle the error or assign a default value as appropriate.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you want to ensure that the destination buffer is always null-terminated and avoid undefined behavior, you can use a safer string copy pattern. This also includes null checks:\n\n```cpp\nif (name != nullptr && vendor_name.c_str() != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), len-1);\n    name[len-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: one or both pointers are null\n}\n```\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
