            {
              "id": "d92a9f20-1715-3246-9d9a-e502e22a5f68",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string from a C++ string's c_str() method into a destination buffer using strncpy, but there are no explicit checks to ensure that either the source (description.c_str()) or the destination (policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description) is non-null before the operation. If either pointer is null, this can result in a crash or other undefined behavior. The risk is further increased because the code does not explicitly assign a null terminator after the copy, and there is no guard to ensure the pointers are valid. The probability of this being a false positive is low, as the analysis has identified a real risk based on the absence of null checks and the use of potentially null pointers.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, add explicit checks to ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null. This prevents undefined behavior if either pointer is invalid. Also, ensure the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (policy && description.c_str()) {\n    strncpy(policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description, description.c_str(), RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1);\n    policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description[RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the copy only occurs if both pointers are valid, and the destination buffer is always null-terminated, preventing buffer overflows and string-related bugs.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to handle the case where either pointer is null more gracefully, you can log an error or return early to avoid undefined behavior:\n\n```cpp\nif (!policy) {\n    // Handle error: policy is null\n    return;\n}\nif (!description.c_str()) {\n    // Handle error: description is null\n    return;\n}\nstrncpy(policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description, description.c_str(), RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1);\npolicy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description[RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis ensures that the function does not proceed with invalid pointers, reducing the risk of crashes or security issues.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
