            {
              "id": "b659a325-6e05-39b0-ba11-4ac3a0c21996",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the code copies a string from a source pointer to a destination buffer using a function that does not check for null pointers. Both the source (the result of calling the string's c_str() method) and the destination (a field within a structure) may be null, as there are no explicit checks to ensure they are valid before the copy operation. If either pointer is null, this can result in undefined behavior, such as a crash or potential security issue. The risk is further highlighted by the classification indicating undefined behavior due to possible null pointers. The use of a string's c_str() as the source adds a small margin of safety, but without explicit null checks, the risk remains. The absence of a guard condition or explicit null-termination after the copy further increases the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy operation, ensure that both the destination and source pointers are not null. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (policy && description.c_str() && policy->policies && policy->num_supported < MAX_POLICIES) {\n    strncpy(policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description, description.c_str(), RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1);\n    policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description[RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis code checks that the main structure, the array, and the string pointer are all valid before copying. It also ensures the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy, which is a best practice when using strncpy.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to handle the case where the source string may be null, you can provide a default value or skip the copy:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src = description.c_str();\nif (policy && policy->policies && policy->num_supported < MAX_POLICIES) {\n    if (src) {\n        strncpy(policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description, src, RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1);\n        policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description[RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1] = '\\0';\n    } else {\n        policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description[0] = '\\0'; // Set as empty string\n    }\n}\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 3\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
