            {
              "id": "0c3f9dc9-a2e1-3d7c-92c3-292b68af3001",
              "name": "Detected Vulnerability in C Function (strlen)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to but not including the first null character. If the string does not have a null character, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters one, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding one at the end of your string. Additionally, you should avoid using `strlen` on strings that come from untrusted sources, as they may not be properly null-terminated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the given code snippet, `strlen(schema_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow if `schema_key` is not null-terminated. A safer alternative would be to use the `std::string::length` function, which is a member function of the `std::string` class and does not rely on null-termination:\n\n```cpp\nfeature_line.substr(schema_key.length()).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: This library is required for the `std::string` class and its member functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "LOW",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-119"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "51972bad-11a3-33a2-a9c7-7b7177282a69",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible null pointer dereference, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The code copies a string from a source that may be null (model_name.c_str()) to a destination that may also be null (cpu_info->model_name), with no explicit checks to ensure either pointer is valid before the operation. The use of a constant for the maximum string length does not mitigate the risk of null pointer dereference. Additionally, there are no explicit null-termination guarantees after the copy, and no guard conditions are present to validate the pointers. This combination of factors means the code is at risk of crashing or exhibiting undefined behavior if either pointer is null at runtime.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that both the destination structure pointer and the source string pointer are not null. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info && cpu_info->model_name && !model_name.empty()) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the destination and source are valid before copying, and also explicitly null-terminates the destination buffer to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the destination buffer is part of a structure and may not be initialized, add a check for the structure pointer and the buffer itself. Also, always null-terminate the destination buffer after copying:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info && cpu_info->model_name) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.empty() ? \"\" : model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach ensures that even if the source string is empty, the destination buffer is safely initialized and null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
