            {
              "id": "05f0bc09-00f8-3a93-a519-2fba50f2386c",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string from a C++ string object to a structure member using a fixed maximum length, but there are no explicit checks to ensure that the destination structure pointer or the source string pointer are valid (non-null) before the copy operation. If either pointer is null, this could result in a crash or other undefined behavior. The use of a C++ string's c_str() method as the source is generally safe, but the absence of pointer validation for both the destination and source increases the risk. The probability of this being a false positive is low because the analysis has detected a real risk of dereferencing null pointers.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy, add explicit checks to ensure that both the destination structure pointer and the source string pointer are not null. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (info != nullptr && !driver_name.empty()) {\n    strncpy(info->driver_name, driver_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1);\n    info->driver_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the copy only occurs if both pointers are valid, and also guarantees that the destination buffer is null-terminated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the destination structure pointer or the source string pointer could be null, handle these cases gracefully by returning an error or taking appropriate action before attempting the copy.\n\n```cpp\nif (info == nullptr) {\n    // Handle error: destination is null\n    return ERROR_INVALID_POINTER;\n}\nif (driver_name.empty()) {\n    info->driver_name[0] = '\\0'; // Set as empty string\n} else {\n    strncpy(info->driver_name, driver_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1);\n    info->driver_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures robust error handling and prevents undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-120",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-476",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html"
