            {
              "id": "6420b844-b1a1-3469-bf3d-8f41319de2d7",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The destination buffer is accessed through a pointer, and there is no explicit check to ensure it is not null, which increases the risk. Additionally, dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for strncpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. There is also no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. These factors indicate a real risk that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are distinct. Also, check that the destination pointer is not null before copying.\n\n```cpp\nif (info &&\n    (reinterpret_cast<const void *>(info->part_number) + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= reinterpret_cast<const void *>(vbios.vbios_pn) ||\n     reinterpret_cast<const void *>(vbios.vbios_pn) + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= reinterpret_cast<const void *>(info->part_number))) {\n    strncpy(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the memory regions do not overlap and that the destination is valid. It also guarantees null-termination of the destination buffer.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping regions. Also, ensure the destination is not null and explicitly null-terminate the buffer after copying.\n\n```cpp\nif (info) {\n    memmove(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis change eliminates the risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory and ensures the destination buffer is properly null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
