            {
              "id": "13437bfe-bd00-3bcd-b8e7-a062cdad5011",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the buffer copy operation. The code copies data from one buffer to another using a function that is not safe when the source and destination overlap. The analysis determined that such overlap is feasible based on data flow between the source and destination. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the destination pointer is not null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. The destination buffer's size is not explicitly known, and there is no evidence that the length argument is derived from the actual buffer capacity. These factors all contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue that could result in memory corruption or program crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure that the memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, handle the copy safely or log an error. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (info->version != reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str)) {\n    strncpy(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap: either skip, log, or use a safe copy\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if overlap between the source and destination cannot be ruled out, use a function that is safe for overlapping memory regions, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. For example:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
