            {
              "id": "575ef44a-f2da-33c7-b357-78915bf2cf8b",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions. The analysis detected that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which can cause unpredictable results when using functions like strncpy. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the destination pointer is not null, which could also lead to a crash. The presence of a NUL terminator assignment after the call is good practice, but it does not mitigate the risk of undefined behavior from overlapping memory. The destination is a pointer type, and there is no evidence that the length argument is derived from the actual buffer capacity, further increasing the risk. All these factors confirm that this is a genuine and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are distinct. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif ((info->build_date + (AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1) <= reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date)) ||\n    (reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date) + (AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1) <= info->build_date)) {\n    strncpy(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    info->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap error, e.g., log or return an error code\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs if the memory regions do not overlap, preventing undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping regions. For example:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ninfo->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\nThis change ensures that the copy is performed safely even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
