            {
              "id": "1e8a628d-b4db-3245-9bb3-f031f781c813",
              "name": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's capacity is not known or proven to be sufficient for the number of bytes being copied. The copy operation uses a length value taken directly from a structure member (header->record_length) without any visible checks to ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to accommodate this amount of data. There are no explicit bounds checks or guards present before the copy, and the destination is a pointer type with no associated size information. The source and destination do not overlap, and both are non-null, but the lack of capacity validation for the destination makes this a high-risk buffer overflow scenario.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the destination buffer has enough space to accommodate the number of bytes specified by the source's record length. Add a check to compare the available space in the destination buffer with the value of `header->record_length`. If the destination buffer is an array or has a known size, use that size for the comparison. For example:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t dest_capacity = /* actual size of cper_data from context */;\nif (header->record_length <= dest_capacity - data_idx) {\n    std::memcpy(&cper_data[data_idx], reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header), header->record_length);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: insufficient buffer size\n}\n```\nIf the size of the destination buffer is not known, refactor the code to ensure that this information is available at the copy site, or use safer abstractions that track buffer sizes.\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "LOW",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fdfffba2-9db7-36d8-838c-34e418c6b73e",
              "name": "Detected Vulnerability in C Function (strlen)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Be careful when manipulating strings, especially when using functions that do not automatically null-terminate strings. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter to prevent reading past the end of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nlen = strlen(str);\n```\n\nConsider using `strnlen` which takes a maximum length parameter:\n\n```cpp\nlen = strnlen(str, MAX_LEN);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LEN` is the maximum expected length of your string. This will prevent `strnlen` from reading past the end of the string if it is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-126",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "LOW",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-126"
                ]
              }
