            {
              "id": "06b1e788-5be4-3467-aaae-0a2c320ca462",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null pointer being passed as the source string. The verdict for this call site is also marked as genuine. The source string is obtained via a method that may return a null pointer if the string is empty or uninitialized, and there are no explicit checks to ensure the source is not null before copying. The destination appears to be a fixed-size buffer within a structure, so it is unlikely to be null. The copy length is determined by the minimum of a maximum length and the source string's length, but this does not mitigate the risk of a null source pointer. No explicit null-termination is enforced after the copy, which could also lead to string handling issues. The absence of overlap between source and destination reduces the risk of memory corruption, but the lack of null checks for the source pointer is a significant concern.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the source string pointer is not null. If the source is null, either skip the copy or assign a safe default value to the destination buffer. Also, ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy to prevent unterminated string issues.\n\n```cpp\nif (name.c_str() != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(info.name, name.c_str(), std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, name.length()));\n    info.name[std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1, name.length())] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    info.name[0] = '\\0'; // or assign a default value\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, use a safer string copy approach that handles null pointers and always null-terminates the destination buffer. For example, use snprintf, which will write a null terminator as long as the buffer size is greater than zero.\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(info.name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, \"%s\", name.c_str() ? name.c_str() : \"\");\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  },
