            {
              "id": "7a0fbe1c-3fe0-3309-b9b3-151c1cb05f8e",
              "name": "Detected Prohibited C Function (strcpy) Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a common source of security vulnerabilities. It copies a string from one location to another, but it does not check the size of the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow, which can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\nThis vulnerability is known as \"Detected Prohibited C Function (strcpy) Vulnerability\". It is a serious issue because it can lead to unexpected behavior, crashes, and security breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcpy` and instead use safer alternatives that check the size of the destination buffer. These include functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info.container_name, container_id.c_str(), sizeof(info.container_name) - 1);\ninfo.container_name[sizeof(info.container_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code uses `strncpy` to copy the string and ensures that the destination buffer is not overflowed. It also manually null-terminates the string, as `strncpy` does not do this if the source string is too long.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncpy` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-120",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-120"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dcaa322b-51f1-3eac-865d-71f4e376fb43",
              "name": "Detected Use of Prohibited C Function (strcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to crashes, data corruption, and potentially code execution, making it a serious security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter. This allows the function to ensure that it does not write past the end of the buffer. Functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy` can be used instead, but they must be used correctly to ensure safety.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use `strncpy` like this:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[12];\nstrncpy(name, \"PLDM_BUNDLE\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nThis code will copy at most `sizeof(name)` characters from the source string to the destination buffer, ensuring that it does not write past the end of the buffer. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated, which `strncpy` does not do if the source string is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-119"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ab95407d-938f-3b33-b975-318b29502b29",
              "name": "Detected Use of Prohibited C Function (strcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. The problem with `strcpy` is that it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to various security issues such as code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcpy` and instead use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions also have their own issues and should be used carefully.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `strcpy(name, \"\")` with `strncpy(name, \"\", sizeof(name))`. This will ensure that no more than `sizeof(name)` characters are copied, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
