              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `snprintf`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to ensure that the size argument correctly represents the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function, such as `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[8];\nstrncpy(name, \"ID_ASD\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIn this example, `strncpy` will copy at most `sizeof(name)` characters. If \"ID_ASD\" is shorter than this, the remaining space in `name` will be filled with '\\0'. The last line ensures that `name` is null-terminated, which is necessary because `strncpy` does not null-terminate the destination string if the source string is too long.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library, which provides the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
