              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions require you to specify the maximum number of characters to be copied, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[6];\nstrncpy(name, \"MMSCH\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` will copy at most `sizeof(name)` characters from the source string to the destination buffer. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated, which `strncpy` does not do if the source string is larger than or equal to the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library, which provides the `strncpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-119"
                ]
              }
