                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to crashes, data corruption, and potentially code execution, making it a serious security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcpy` and instead use safer alternatives that check the size of the destination buffer. These include functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions also have their own caveats and must be used correctly to avoid vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[10];\nstrncpy(name, \"DMCU_ERAM\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` is used to copy the string. The `sizeof(name)` argument ensures that no more characters than the size of the destination buffer are copied. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated, which `strncpy` does not do if the source string is larger than the size argument.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
