              "id": "3baba8f8-759a-3970-a5ba-39e8570654b0",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation where neither the source nor the destination pointers are explicitly checked for null before the operation. The classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior if either pointer is null. The source is a C-style string, and the destination is a character pointer with no known capacity, and there are no guards or null-termination after the copy. The analysis also notes that both the source and destination may be null at this point in the code, which could lead to a crash or other unpredictable behavior. The absence of overlap risk and the use of a constant macro for the copy length do not mitigate the null pointer risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy, add explicit checks to ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (market_name != nullptr && empty.c_str() != nullptr) {\n    std::strncpy(market_name, empty.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nIf either pointer can be null as part of normal program logic, handle the error or assign a safe default value as appropriate.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you want to ensure that the destination buffer is always null-terminated, add an explicit null-terminator after the copy. This is especially important because `strncpy` does not guarantee null-termination if the source string is longer than the specified length.\n\n```cpp\nif (market_name != nullptr && empty.c_str() != nullptr) {\n    std::strncpy(market_name, empty.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
