            {
              "id": "93211a61-bb21-3296-870b-b57fa97344cb",
              "name": "\"Detected Use of Prohibited C Function (strcpy)\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to crashes, data corruption, and potentially code execution, making it a serious security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions allow you to specify the maximum number of characters to be copied, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use `strncpy` like this:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[12];\nstrncpy(name, \"CP_MEC_JT1\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nOr `strlcpy` like this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <bsd/string.h>\n\nchar name[12];\nstrlcpy(name, \"CP_MEC_JT1\", sizeof(name));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are required. If you choose to use `strlcpy`, you will need to include the `<bsd/string.h>` header file, which is part of the BSD library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-119"
                ]
              }
