              "name": "\"Detected Use of Prohibited C Function (strcpy)\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `snprintf`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to ensure that the size argument correctly reflects the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function like `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[10];\nstrncpy(name, \"CP_PFP\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` will copy at most `sizeof(name)` characters from the source string to the destination buffer. If the source string is larger, it will be truncated. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated, which `strncpy` does not guarantee.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
