              "id": "6dd7f938-a4ed-3f9d-8c72-ba0e18fc7a00",
              "name": "Problematic C function detected (malloc)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is used as the destination in a strcpy call immediately after allocation, without any check for a NULL return value. The absence of a null guard before the first use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The allocation size is not a simple sizeof(T) pattern, and there are no zero or overflow guards present. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is returned and passed to another call, but this does not affect the immediate risk of dereferencing a potentially null pointer. These factors indicate a genuine vulnerability that could lead to a crash or undefined behavior if memory allocation fails.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nchar *dev_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(dev_name, src);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check immediately after the allocation:\n\n```c\nchar *dev_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (dev_name == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return an error or set an error code\n    return NULL;\n}\nstrcpy(dev_name, src);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to a crash or undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
