            {
              "id": "d978da02-dbc8-38d8-8dd5-346f056d7e6a",
              "name": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible null pointer dereference, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The source of the copy operation is a C-style string obtained from a string object, but there is no explicit check to ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the memory copy. The destination is a character array, which is less likely to be null, but the absence of a guard for the source pointer increases the risk. Additionally, the presence of an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy does not mitigate the risk of dereferencing a null pointer. Therefore, this issue should be addressed to prevent potential crashes or undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source string object is not empty or null. Add a check to verify that the source pointer returned by `c_str()` is not null. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (!newString.empty()) {\n    std::memcpy(buffer, newString.c_str(), copy_len);\n    buffer[copy_len] = '\\0'; // Assuming this is the explicit NUL assignment\n} else {\n    // Handle the empty string case appropriately\n    buffer[0] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-476",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "LOW",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-119",
                  "CWE-476"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7b18c86f-cfd1-3ef6-b2c5-a438c4b55d11",
              "name": "\"Detected Use of Prohibited C Function (strcpy)\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to crashes, data corruption, and potentially code execution, making it a serious security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter. This allows the function to ensure that it does not write past the end of the buffer. Examples of such functions include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to ensure that the length parameter is correctly calculated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[10];\nstrcpy(name, \"SMU\");\n```\n\nYou should use:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[10];\nstrncpy(name, \"SMU\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-119"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e60adcc9-a0be-3b2b-8d85-28095efe06bb",
              "name": "Detected Use of Prohibited C Function (strcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a variety of security issues, including arbitrary code execution, data corruption, and application crashes.\n\nThe vulnerability sink in question is `strcpy(dev_name, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA)`. Here, `strcpy` is used to copy the string `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` into `dev_name`. If `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` is larger than `dev_name`, this will result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with a safer function that checks the size of the destination buffer. This includes functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions have their own caveats and should be used carefully.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(dev_name, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA, sizeof(dev_name));\ndev_name[sizeof(dev_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` is used to copy the string `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` into `dev_name`. The `sizeof(dev_name)` argument ensures that no more than the size of `dev_name` characters are copied. The second line ensures that `dev_name` is null-terminated, which `strncpy` does not guarantee.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-120",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-120"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "42e975d8-9304-3dd4-b26c-2abd9fceca79",
              "name": "Detected Prohibited C Function",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. In the provided code, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` and similar functions that do not perform bounds checking or format string checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have these issues. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `fprintf` to the standard output:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n\nfprintf(stdout, \"AMDSMI, Success, %s found \\\"%s\\\" and returns:%d\\n\", driver_name, file_name, GOAMDSMI_STATUS_SUCCESS);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: This is the C++ version of the C Standard Input and Output Library. It provides functions to perform input and output operations.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-676",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-676"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "363a6512-9995-32d0-a44f-b60608b1d96b",
              "name": "Detected Prohibited C Function",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In the provided code, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using dangerous C functions like `printf`. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have these vulnerabilities. For example, in C++, you can use the `iostream` library for output, which is type-safe and does not have format string vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `iostream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string driver_name;\nstd::string file_name;\nint GOAMDSMI_STATUS_FAILURE;\n\nstd::cout << \"AMDSMI, Status, \" << driver_name << \" not found, missing \\\"\" << file_name << \"\\\" and returns:\" << GOAMDSMI_STATUS_FAILURE << std::endl;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe original code requires the `stdio.h` library for the `printf` function. The fixed code requires the `iostream` and `string` libraries.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-134",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-134"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
